 "Cairnpapple Hill": {
  "type": "site",
  "id": "c59b8485-583b-4c64-8d5b-61e046ed3437",
  "name": "Cairnpapple Hill",
  "country": "Scotland",
  "site_type": "Cairn",
  "period_name": "4500 - 3000 BC",
  "period_start": -4000,
  "period_end": null,
  "description": "Cairnpapple Hill is a hill with a dominating position in central lowland Scotland with views from coast to coast [1]. It was used and re-used as a major ritual site for around 4,000 years [1]. In the 19th century the site was completely concealed by trees, then in 1947–1948 excavations by Stuart Piggott found a series of ritual monuments from successive prehistoric periods [1]. Neolithic rituals began around 3500 BC with signs of small hearths, and precious objects left on the hill, presumably as offerings [1]. Probably around 3000 BC a Class II henge was constructed with the hilltop being surrounded by a bank outside a ditch about 12 ft wide cut over 3 ft into the rock [1]. Some time later a Bronze Age ritual added a small stone and clay cairn just off-centre inside the monument [1]. Under the cairn traces were found of at least one burial, with wooden objects (perhaps a mask and club) and beaker people style pottery which indicates a date around 2000 BC [1].",
  "lat": 55.928223871062734,
  "lon": -3.62259656202852,
  "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cairnpapple_Hill",
  "best_wiki_url": null,
